 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "In a parallel world, the analogous hypothesis has been proved for decades.",
   "start": 0.725,
   "end": 5.425
  },
  {
   "text": "Do arithmetic on a clock with p hours, p prime,",
   "start": 5.425,
   "end": 9.025
  },
  {
   "text": "and count the solutions of y² + y = x³ − x.",
   "start": 9.025,
   "end": 13.75
  },
  {
   "text": "For p equal to thirteen, there are fifteen, plus one point at infinity.",
   "start": 13.75,
   "end": 18.825
  },
  {
   "text": "You'd expect about p plus one.",
   "start": 19.75,
   "end": 21.775
  },
  {
   "text": "The surprise is how close it stays: the error is never more than two root p,",
   "start": 21.775,
   "end": 26.5
  },
  {
   "text": "for every prime.",
   "start": 26.5,
   "end": 27.938
  },
  {
   "text": "Hasse proved this for curves like this one in the 1930s,",
   "start": 27.938,
   "end": 31.613
  },
  {
   "text": "Weil for all curves in the 1940s, and Deligne went much further in 1974.",
   "start": 31.613,
   "end": 36.938
  },
  {
   "text": "Each such equation has a zeta function,",
   "start": 36.938,
   "end": 39.325
  },
  {
   "text": "and this bound is exactly its Riemann Hypothesis.",
   "start": 39.325,
   "end": 42.575
  }
 ],
